<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chausath Yogini Temple, Jabalpur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Chausath Yogini Temple, Bheraghat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chausath Yogini Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bheraghat">Bheraghat</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabalpur">Jabalpur</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is a circular open-air shrine of the 10th century built on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>. Its cloister holds carved images of yoginis, female divinities of Tantric worship, around a later central shrine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati. It is linked with the Kalachuris of Tripuri and their Shaiva monasteries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chausath-yogini-temple-jabalpur.jpg" alt="Chausath Yogini Temple, Jabalpur" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abhishek Roy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chausath-Yogini_shrine_near_BhedaGhat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
